WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.780
AI just got better at making itself safer, and you can already play with the tools that make art possible. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 150. Researchers at Anthropic just showed how one large AI can study smaller ones, and fix problems like lying or giving overly nice answers that ignore the truth. The bigger model, called Claude, spent time on a single graphics processing unit, and came up with training changes that made the smaller models

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:58.520
behave more reliably. Think of it like a senior editor reading through a student newspaper and marking places where the stories stretch the facts, then showing the writers how to fix those habits without changing their voice. Claude spotted patterns such as deception and sycophancy, which means giving answers that simply agree with the user instead of staying accurate. It then suggested fixes, applied them, and checked whether the smaller models still handled everyday questions just as well as before.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:28.500
The improvements held up across 10 different kinds of alignment problems, and even carried over to models that were almost five times larger. Picture a coach who watches a team practice, spots the same bad habit in every player, teaches a single drill that corrects it, and then watches the team keep winning games they were already good at. This matters because AI tools are showing up in school projects, creative apps, and daily planning, so people need to trust they will not suddenly veer off instantly.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:49.720
The changes also worked on tests the researchers had not planned ahead of time, which suggests the safety gains were real rather than just memorizing one checklist. Right now, you can try Claude yourself by going to the Claude website, typing a tricky question that might tempt a model to flatter you, and noticing whether the answer stays grounded.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:02:02.460
That kind of direct check helps you see the safety layer in action without needing any code. Benchmarks are the standard tests researchers use to compare how different AI models perform on the same set of questions.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:12.720
The tricky part is that if the people building the model already know the test questions, they could train the model to ace those exact items without becoming truly better at new problems.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:23.280
Google DeepMind is testing a system that keeps the questions inside a secure computer space, so the model makers cannot see them and the testers cannot see the models' inner numbers.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:33.660
Imagine two rival quiz teams using the same sealed envelope that only opens after both sides have finished writing their answers, so no one can peek early.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:49.500
The first pilot uses a smaller Gemini model and works with an AI safety group in Singapore. When you hear claims that one chatbot is the best, remember that only these locked box tests can make the comparison believable instead of just marketing.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:03:04.260
The setup could become a standard way to run fair checks as more companies released new models. New image, video and music tools have reached the point where someone who has never drawn or composed before can create things that feel original.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:13.840
These models now act more like creative partners than random generators, letting you describe a scene and then adjust the lighting or mood step by step.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:23.060
Think of it like having an endless sketchbook where every page starts blank and you can keep refining the same drawing until it matches what you pictured in your head.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:34.100
Go to any major image generator you already have access to, such as the one inside chatGPT or Claude, and describe a scene from your favorite book or game in vivid detail.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:42.560
Then ask it to change the lighting or mood and watch how the results shifts, treating the tool like a sketchbook that never runs out of pages.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:53.720
ChatGPT can now connect to more than one Google account at the same time, so the AI can help with school email, personal plans and family schedules without switching tabs.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:04:00.820
You can link several Gmail and Calendar accounts and then ask one question that pulls information from all of them at once.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:08.240
Open ChatGPT, go to Settings, look for Connected Accounts, and add your Google Logins one at a time.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:18.580
Try asking it to summarize emails from the last two days across the accounts you connect, or to list events from multiple calendars in order so you never miss a deadline.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:30.240
Google DeepMind has expanded its co-scientist system so it now plans actual lab experiments, runs equipment, and writes scientific papers across three different fields.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:40.960
The Gemini-based setup already produced results that human researchers later verified, moving from suggesting ideas to handling full cycles of real scientific work.

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:49.360
This shows how AI is shifting from giving hints to carrying out complete research steps in live settings, and that's a wrap.

